<DocsLayout title="Advanced OpenAI Agents SDK guide | Agent-Gantry" description="Approvals, per-turn selection internals, tracing and error modes for the Agent-Gantry OpenAI Agents SDK integration.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Advanced framework guide</p>
  <h1>Advanced OpenAI Agents SDK</h1>
  <p class="lead">Human approval, how per-turn selection works inside a run, tracing, and the older per-run helpers.</p>
  <p><a class="pill" href="/Agent-Gantry/docs/frameworks/openai-agents/">← Basic OpenAI Agents SDK usage</a></p>

  <h2>Human approval</h2>
  <p>A tool registered with <code>requires_confirmation=True</code>, or matched by a <code>SecurityPolicy(require_confirmation=[...])</code> pattern, sets the SDK's <code>needs_approval</code>. The run pauses with <code>result.interruptions</code>; approving replays the call once through <code>gantry.execute</code> with only the confirmation gate cleared. A rejected call never runs.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`result = await Runner.run(agent, "Refund order 4471")
if result.interruptions:
    state = result.to_state()
    for item in result.interruptions:
        if ask_a_human(item):
            state.approve(item)
        else:
            state.reject(item)
    result = await Runner.run(agent, state)`} />
  <p><code>gantry.requires_confirmation(tool)</code> answers the same question without running anything. A custom policy that decides only at call time still blocks the call; the model is told it needs approval.</p>

  <h2>Per-turn selection inside a run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> <code>dynamic_tools()</code> returns the catalogue as <code>FunctionTool</code>s whose <code>is_enabled</code> callbacks share one selector. The SDK evaluates them at the start of each turn; the first evaluation runs one Gantry selection and the rest reuse it.</li>
    <li><strong>Query:</strong> the newest user message in the run's input, or after a Gantry tool call that message plus the tool's output (first 2,000 characters). <code>query_from_results=False</code> keeps tool output out of retrieval.</li>
    <li><strong>State:</strong> cached per run and keyed to the run, so concurrent runs of one agent are independent and <code>agent.tools</code> is never rewritten.</li>
    <li><strong>Stickiness:</strong> tools offered earlier in a run stay offered, up to <code>max_offered</code> (default <code>3 * limit</code>); the current selection and pinned tools always stay. Use <code>sticky=False</code> for a strict top-k.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure:</strong> if retrieval fails mid-run, the turn keeps the run's earlier tools and logs a warning.</li>
    <li><strong>New tools:</strong> tools registered after <code>dynamic_tools()</code> is called are not in the catalogue; call it again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Older per-run helpers</h2>
  <p><code>adapter.session(agent)</code>, <code>adapter.run(agent, input)</code> and <code>adapter.run_hooks(agent)</code> re-select by rewriting <code>agent.tools</code>. They still work and gain the same error and approval behaviour, but because they mutate the agent, use them for one run at a time per agent. <code>dynamic_tools()</code> has no such restriction.</p>

  <h2>Tracing</h2>
  <p>When the SDK is tracing, its current span becomes the Gantry <code>ToolCall</code>'s <code>trace_id</code> and <code>parent_span_id</code>; otherwise an active OpenTelemetry span is used. <code>gantry.on_tool_call</code> events then line up with the agent's trace.</p>

  <h2>Production checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Handle <code>result.interruptions</code> on any agent that can reach a confirmation-gated tool.</li>
    <li>Use an <code>error_formatter</code> if handler exception messages may contain internals.</li>
    <li>Install <code>agent-gantry[openai-agents]</code> in its own environment, apart from CrewAI.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
